---

<ArticleLayout
    title={title}
    description={description}
    author={author}
    authorUrl={authorUrl}
    pubDate={pubDate}
    readingTime={readingTime}
    image={image}
    imageAlt={imageAlt}
    category="VPN"
    categorySlug="vpn"
    headings={headings}
>
  <!-- Introduction -->
  <h2 id="introduction">The "Free" Trap</h2>
  <p>
    Okay, I get it. You're scrolling the app store and there's like fifty "FREE VPN" options. NordVPN wants $3/month and
    here's some random app promising the same thing for nothing. Why would anyone pay?
  </p>
  <p>
    Here's the thing. I tried this route. Downloaded a few free VPNs back when I was being cheap. Thought I was smart.
  </p>
  <p>
    I wasn't smart.
  </p>
  <p>
    Running VPN servers costs money. Bandwidth costs money. If they're not charging you, they're making money some other
    way. Usually by selling your data. The exact thing a VPN is supposed to prevent.
  </p>

  <!-- How Free VPNs Make Money -->
  <h2 id="how-free-vpns-make-money">How Free VPNs Actually Stay Afloat</h2>
  <p>
    They're businesses, not charities. Here's what's really happening:
  </p>

  <h3>They're Selling Your Data</h3>
  <p>
    Most common model. The VPN sees everything—every site, every search, every login. They package that up and sell it
    to advertisers and data brokers.
  </p>
  <p>
    A 2022 study found 75% of free VPN apps had tracking libraries baked in. That's not a typo. Three out of four.
  </p>

  <h3>Ads Everywhere</h3>
  <p>
    Some inject ads into your browsing. Not just in the app—into websites you visit. I've seen this happen. Sketchy as
    hell.
  </p>

  <h3>Your Internet Becomes Their Exit Node</h3>
  <p>
    This one's wild. Hola VPN got caught turning user devices into exit nodes for their network. Your IP address, other
    people's traffic running through it. One guy's internet connection was used for a botnet. Could've been yours.
  </p>

  <h3>The Legit Model: Freemium</h3>
  <p>
    ProtonVPN and Windscribe do it right. Free tier with limits. Paid users subsidize free users. No shady stuff. This
    is the only model I trust.
  </p>

  <h3>The Simple Question</h3>
  <p>
    How does this company make money? If you can't answer that clearly, assume it's your data.
  </p>

  <!-- Security Risks -->
  <h2 id="security-risks">Security? What Security?</h2>
  <p>
    Beyond the privacy stuff, free VPNs are often actual security risks.
  </p>

  <h3>Malware Built In</h3>
  <p>
    A CSIRO study: 38% of free Android VPN apps contained malware. These apps have deep access to your device. They see
    everything. Some <a href="/guides/phishing-scams-remote-workers">capture passwords</a>. Some install more malware.
  </p>

  <h3>Garbage Encryption</h3>
  <p>
    Some use outdated protocols. Some implement encryption wrong. Either way, someone sophisticated could crack it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Paid VPNs use real standards—AES-256, WireGuard—and have actual security teams. Free VPNs? Maybe one developer who
    also does marketing.
  </p>

  <h3>Leak City</h3>
  <p>
    I've tested free VPNs that leaked my real IP address. The whole point of a VPN, defeated. Your "protected"
    connection showing exactly who you are.
  </p>
  <p>
    Paid VPNs include kill switches and leak protection. Standard features. Free VPNs? Usually don't.
  </p>

  <h3>No Audits</h3>
  <p>
    NordVPN, ExpressVPN, Surfshark—all independently audited. Third parties checking if they actually do what they
    claim.
  </p>
  <p>
    Free VPNs? Never seen one submit to an audit. Wonder why.
  </p>

  <!-- Performance Comparison -->
  <h2 id="performance-comparison">The Numbers Don't Lie</h2>
  <p>
    Here's the reality:
  </p>
  <ComparisonTable
      columns={comparisonColumns}
      rows={comparisonRows}
      caption="Free vs paid VPN comparison"
  />

  <p>
    <strong>Speed:</strong> Free VPNs throttle you. 5-20 Mbps when you could have 80-95 Mbps. Try doing a video call on
    that.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Data:</strong> Most cap you at 500MB-10GB per month. One Zoom meeting burns through a gig. One Netflix movie
    uses 3-7GB. You'd hit the limit before lunch.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Servers:</strong> Free VPNs offer maybe 3-10 locations. Paid ones give you 50-100+ countries. More options,
    less congestion, better speeds.
  </p>
  <p>
    For actual <a href="/tools/best-vpn-digital-nomads">remote work</a>, free VPNs are basically unusable.
  </p>

  <!-- When Free VPNs Might Be Okay -->
  <h2 id="when-free-okay">When Free Might Be Okay</h2>
  <p>
    Look, I'm not saying never. There are situations:
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Quick one-off stuff:</strong> Need to access one region-locked article? Don't need to log into anything
    sensitive? ProtonVPN's free tier is fine.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Testing before buying:</strong> Windscribe and ProtonVPN let you try the apps before committing. That's
    literally why free tiers exist.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Rare public WiFi use:</strong> You work from home 99% of the time? Only <a
      href="/guides/do-i-need-vpn-cafes">hit coffee shops occasionally</a>? A
    free tier's data limit might work.
  </p>

  <h3>When Free Is Never Okay</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Client data. Any client data.</li>
    <li>Banking or financial accounts</li>
    <li><a href="/guides/secure-home-wifi-network">NDAs, contracts, sensitive business stuff</a></li>
    <li>Daily remote work</li>
    <li>Streaming (doesn't work anyway)</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    If your work involves any actual responsibility, pay for the VPN.
  </p>

  <!-- Best Freemium VPNs -->
  <h2 id="best-freemium">The Only Free VPNs I'd Trust</h2>
  <ComparisonTable
      columns={freemiumColumns}
      rows={freemiumRows}
      caption="Best freemium VPN options"
  />

  <h3>ProtonVPN Free</h3>
  <p>
    The gold standard. Swiss company. Same people behind ProtonMail. They've been audited. No logs. No ads. Unlimited
    data—the only free VPN without caps.
  </p>
  <p>
    Downsides: Only 3 countries, medium speeds, no streaming, one device.
  </p>
  <p>
    For basic public WiFi protection, it actually works.
  </p>

  <h3>Windscribe Free</h3>
  <p>
    10GB/month. Generous. 10 countries available. Sometimes works with Netflix.
  </p>
  <p>
    Tweet about them and you get 5GB more. Confirm your email, another 1GB. They're transparent about the hustle.
  </p>

  <h3>hide.me Free</h3>
  <p>
    10GB/month, 8 countries. Clear about their model. No-logs policy.
  </p>
  <p>
    Solid option if ProtonVPN doesn't work for you.
  </p>

  <!-- Why Paid VPNs Are Worth It -->
  <h2 id="why-paid-worth-it">Why I Pay (And You Should Too)</h2>

  <h3>Trust</h3>
  <p>
    Paid VPNs make money by keeping you safe. That's the business model. Our interests align.
  </p>

  <h3>It Just Works</h3>
  <p>
    Unlimited data. Fast speeds. Thousands of servers. I forget it's even running most days. No buffering, no failed
    calls, no upload issues.
  </p>

  <h3>Streaming</h3>
  <p>
    <a href="/tools/best-vpn-streaming-travel">Netflix works. Disney+ works. BBC iPlayer works.</a> Try doing that with
    a free VPN. (You can't.)
  </p>

  <h3>Support</h3>
  <p>
    24/7 chat. Problems actually get solved. My free VPN phase? Support was a Reddit thread from 2019.
  </p>

  <h3>Perspective</h3>
  <p>
    $3-5/month. That's one fancy coffee. Compare that to:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Compromised client data? Career-ending.</li>
    <li>Identity theft? Thousands of dollars, months of hassle.</li>
    <li>Hours wasted on slow free VPN? Time you don't get back.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The math is obvious.
  </p>

  <!-- Budget-Friendly Paid Options -->
  <h2 id="budget-options">Budget Options That Actually Work</h2>
  <p>
    You don't need expensive.
  </p>
  <ComparisonTable
      columns={budgetColumns}
      rows={budgetRows}
      caption="Best budget VPN options"
  />

  <h3>Surfshark — $2.49/mo</h3>
  <p>
    Best value out there. Unlimited devices. Netflix works. Less than a coffee.
  </p>
  <AffiliateButton
      href="/go/surfshark"
      text="Get Surfshark — $2.49/mo (81% Off)"
      variant="primary"
      program="surfshark"
  />

  <h3>NordVPN — $3.39/mo</h3>
  <p>
    What I use. Fast, works everywhere, extra security features. Worth the extra dollar.
  </p>
  <AffiliateButton
      href="/go/nordvpn"
      text="Get NordVPN — $3.39/mo (68% Off)"
      variant="primary"
      program="nordvpn"
  />

  <h3>PIA — $2.19/mo</h3>
  <p>
    Cheapest option that's legit. Not as polished, but solid protection.
  </p>
  <AffiliateButton
      href="/go/pia"
      text="Get PIA — $2.19/mo (83% Off)"
      variant="secondary"
      program="pia"
  />
  <p>
    All of them require 2-year commitments for these prices. Monthly is way more expensive.
  </p>

  <!-- FAQ Section -->
  <FAQSection faqs={faqs}/>

  <!-- Recommendation -->
  <h2 id="recommendation">The Bottom Line</h2>
  <p>
    For remote work: pay for a VPN. $3/month. Skip one coffee.
  </p>
  <p>
    My pick: NordVPN. Works everywhere, fast, doesn't annoy me.
  </p>
  <p>
    Budget tight? Surfshark at $2.49.
  </p>
  <p>
    Must use free? ProtonVPN. It's the only one I trust.
  </p>
  <p>
    Never use random app store VPNs. The risks aren't worth zero dollars.
  </p>
  <p>
    30-day money-back guarantees on all the paid options. Try one. <a href="/tools/how-to-setup-vpn-all-devices">Actually
    test it</a>. Then decide.
  </p>

  <AffiliateButton
      href="/go/nordvpn"
      text="Get NordVPN — 30-Day Money-Back Guarantee"
      variant="primary"
      program="nordvpn"
  />

  <!-- Related Articles -->
  <Fragment slot="full-width">
    <RelatedArticles articles={relatedArticles}/>
  </Fragment>
</ArticleLayout>
